#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055877} :activity/id #uuid "16bd6886-0670-51ea-9c62-c70c7e015ce4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050088 :topic/id #uuid "2c984580-e283-5888-86e7-f792567f1a8a" :topic/math-academy-id 55} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055877 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056003 :tutorial/content "Interesting mathematical problems often involve a large (or infinite) collection of sets. Labeling each set with a subscript is often convenient in such situations. \n\nFor example, suppose we define a collection of sets $A_i$ for $i\\in\\{1,2,3,\\ldots\\} = \\mathbb N$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA_1 = \\{1,2\\}, \\qquad A_2 = \\{3,4\\}, \\qquad A_3 = \\{5,6\\}, \\qquad \\ldots\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- This collection of $A_i$'s, which we denote $\\big\\{ A_i \\big\\}_{i \\in \\mathbb N},$ is called a **family of indexed sets.**\n\n- The set $i=\\{1,2,3,\\ldots\\} = \\mathbb N$ is called the **index set**.\n\nWriting each set with a subscript makes talking about a general $A_i$ easier. In this particular case, for example, we can express a general member of this family in terms of its index:\n\n$$\nA_i = \\{2i-1,\\, 2i\\}, \\qquad i\\in\\mathbb N\n$$\n\nSo, if we wanted to write down the elements of $A_{100},$ we substitute $i=100$ into the definition of $A_i{:}$\n\n$$\nA_{100} = \\big\\{2(100)-1,\\, 2(100)\\big\\} = \\{199,200\\}\n$$\n\nWe can use any index set when defining a family of indexed sets. \n\nFor example, consider the indexed family $\\big\\{ B_x \\big\\}_{x \\in \\mathbb R},$ where $B_x$ is the real interval defined by\n\n$$\nB_x = \\big[ 0, x^2 \\big), \\qquad x \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nHere, the index set is $\\mathbb{R}.$ \n\nTo write down, say, the elements of $B_{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{5}},$ we substitute $x={\\color{blue}\\sqrt{5}}$ into our definition of $B_x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}_{\\sqrt{5}} & =[0,\\,(\\sqrt{5})^{2}) \\\\  & =[0,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "94ce30ce-17fc-5813-9947-89a8d392343b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7957 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9a6cdf07-394b-572b-b1c9-fc2d60400ed0" :step/math-academy-id 70625 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055878}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055878 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054831 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054362 :question/id #uuid "154d4cf7-a72a-51b1-bbb3-9bcf83b88e98" :question/math-academy-id "e-12665"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f986c29-111e-5cb3-8104-62ab734f1c47" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284706}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of an Indexed Family"} :step/id #uuid "0b2b9b72-b07b-51b4-970f-a98643e205c5" :step/math-academy-id 70626 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055879}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055879 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056033 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the indexed family $\\{A_i\\}_{i\\in I}$ where $I$ is an index set. \n\nThe **union** of a family of indexed sets is the set containing all elements belonging to *at least one* set in the indexed family:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{ x\\in U \\: : \\: x \\in A_i \\:\\:\\textrm{for at least one } A_i,\\:\\: i \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following family of indexed sets:\n\n$$\nX_i = \\{ -i, \\,0, \\, i \\}, \\qquad i\\in \\mathbb N\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the following union:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in \\mathbb N} X_i\n$$\n\nTo calculate our union, let's write down a few of our indexed sets:\n\n- If $i=1,$ we have $X_1 = \\{ -1, 0, 1 \\}.$\n\n- If $i=2,$ we have $X_2 = \\{ -2, 0, 2 \\}.$\n\n- If $i=3,$ we have $X_3 = \\{ -3, 0, 3 \\}.$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on. \n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- It's clear that the $X_i$'s contain only integers. Therefore, any union of $X_i$'s will contain only integers. So, \n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in \\mathbb N} X_i  \\subseteq \\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\n- Notice that *any* integer $n$ belongs to our union. For instance, $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ lies in $X_n=\\{ -n, 0, n \\}$ and, consequently, lies in the union of all $X_i$'s. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in \\mathbb N} X_i  \\supseteq \\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\nThus, using the fact that $A\\subseteq B$ and $B\\subseteq A$ if and only if $A=B,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋃}_{i\\in N}{X}_{i} & =\\overset{{X}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{{-1,0,1}}}\\cup \\overset{{X}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{{-2,0,2}}}\\cup \\overset{{X}_{3}}{\\overset{⏞}{{-3,0,3}}}\\cup ⋯=Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since our index set $I = \\mathbb N$ in this example, we can also use the following notation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋃}_{i=1}^{\\infty }{X}_{i}=Z\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "44240f43-3346-5c5f-9955-317e4e8ad24c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8177 :tutorial/title "Unions of Indexed  Families"} :step/id #uuid "2ec1fad8-799b-53d9-878f-444b40b1e568" :step/math-academy-id 70630 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055880}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055880 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056034 :tutorial/content "Similarly, the **intersection** of a family of indexed sets $\\{A_i\\}_{i\\in I}$ contains all elements that belong to *all* sets in our family:\n\n$$\n\\bigcap_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{ x\\in U \\: : \\: x \\in A_i \\:\\:\\textrm{for all } A_i,\\:\\: i \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following family of indexed sets:\n\n$$\nS_i = \\left[ 0, \\: 10^{-i} \\right], \\qquad i \\in \\mathbb{N}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the following intersection:\n\n$$\n\\bigcap_{i \\in \\mathbb{N}} S_i\n$$\n\nTo calculate our intersection, let's write down a few of our indexed sets:\n\n- If $i=1,$ we have $S_1 = [0, 0.1]$\n\n- If $i=2,$ we have $S_2 = \\left[ 0, 0.01 \\right]$\n\n- If $i=3,$ we have $S_3 = \\left[ 0, 0.001 \\right]$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on.\n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- As $i$ increases, the right endpoint of $S_i$ approaches $0$ but never reaches it.\n\n- The left endpoint equals zero for each $S_i.$\n\nSo, the only element that lies in all sets is $0.$ Therefore, the intersection of our indexed family is the set containing zero only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋂}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}={0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since our index set $I = \\mathbb N$ in this example, we can also use the following notation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋂}_{i=1}^{\\infty }{S}_{i}={0}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "03cd6876-c452-576d-8f1a-24bb0fe077ea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8178 :tutorial/title "Intersections of Indexed  Families"} :step/id #uuid "253c7481-555b-5d32-a3f7-49642ec94a10" :step/math-academy-id 70631 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055881}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055881 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054832 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054369 :question/id #uuid "60e24dac-c47a-53ee-9c91-0e6987e966f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12763"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "957a2f61-61b1-5675-ba84-5348a47af986" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051033}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287881}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unions and Intersections of Finite Families of Indexed Sets"} :step/id #uuid "c74f24a2-dcab-503c-9e0c-8b67b381e9c4" :step/math-academy-id 70632 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055882}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055882 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054833 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054370 :question/id #uuid "842ee2c1-8e78-56dd-b109-b9fcc273704e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12764"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1e93acb-6c0b-5263-9df9-9dcc00680845" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363555}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unions and Intersections of (Countably) Infinite Families of Indexed Sets"} :step/id #uuid "36a1848e-f2f5-52f9-9aad-05995f22fb49" :step/math-academy-id 70636}] :activity/title "Indexed Sets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054911}]}
